  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2},r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{256})}{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{255}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{425}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
